The primary objective of reinforcement learning is to develop a strategy 
that enables an agent to maximize rewards while interacting with an environment. 
In this study, we focus on a specific subset of reinforcement learning problems, 
where agents and environments are model-based and exhibit consistent patterns, 
allowing for accurate prediction of outcomes. We propose a novel approach to 
reinforcement learning, built on the principles of Bayesian inference and 
utilizing a Gaussian process to predict state-to-next-state transitions, 
thereby facilitating the solution of the reinforcement learning problem. 
This method is particularly well-suited for environments that are time-sensitive and 
subject to uncertainty, requiring the agent to balance exploration and 
optimization to achieve success within a set timeframe. The proposed method is 
capable of handling complex tasks, such as sequential decision making, and is 
demonstrated through its successful application to a range of challenging tasks. 
Furthermore, we show that this method is theoretically optimal, achieving the 
highest possible regret upper bound in scenarios with known dynamics and 
homogeneous rewards. The method is also capable of handling uncertainty in the 
environment and is capable of adapting to changes in the environment. 
Experimental results validate the efficacy of the approach and highlight its 
usefulness in a variety of practical applications. The code for the method is 
available online. 
