Mastering complex games like chess and Go has long been an elusive goal for artificial intelligence. Recent breakthroughs in deep reinforcement learning have brought us closer to this goal, but there's an obstacle to overcome – the massive processing power required for these algorithms. This is why distributed reinforcement learning systems are becoming increasingly important. This paper introduces an innovative distributed learning system called Dark Knight, which employs a unique distributed memory reinforcement learning framework. The system is designed to handle distributed systems efficiently and reduce communication costs. By combining two novel techniques – distributed memory search and distributed memory experience – Dark Knight can handle extremely large state spaces and scale well on various environments. The results of our experiments show that Dark Knight outperforms its competitors in several key areas, including memory usage and training efficiency. The benefits of our system are particularly apparent when dealing with complex games like Go and RobotArm, where it can reduce memory usage by 5 times and training time by 1.5 times. The code for our system is publicly available on GitHub. The implementation details are further discussed in the supplementary material. The experimental results and related data are also publicly available. The key contributions of our system are also discussed in further detail within the context of this research paper. The code for our system is available at https://github.com/ustc-ailab/Distributed-DRL. The experimental results and related data are available at https://ustc-ailab.github.io/Distributed-DRL/. The key contributions of our system are also discussed in further detail within the context of this research paper. The code for our system is available at https://github.com/ustc-ailab/Distributed-DRL. The experimental results and related data are available at https://ustc-ailab.github.io/Distributed-DRL/. 
